.TH ATEXIT 3
.SH NAME
atexit \- register a function to run at normal termination
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.B int atexit(void (*func)(void));
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Atexit
records
.I func
to be called when the program terminates normally.  It returns 0 on success and
\-1 if the registration could not be made \- a null
.I func
or more than
.B 32
of them, which is C11 sec 7.22.4.2's own minimum and all this implementation
offers.
.PP
The registered functions are called by
.IR exit (2),
.B in reverse order of registration,
and
.B before
the standard I/O streams are flushed and closed, so a handler may still print.
Each is called with no arguments and its return value is discarded.
.PP
.B It is not reached by every exit.
Nothing runs on
.IR _exit (2),
on a signal that terminates the process, or on a
.I "sys exit"
from the assembler, so it is a way of tidying up after a program's own decision
to stop, not after a crash.  A handler that itself calls
.I exit
is undefined behaviour in C11 and recurses here.
.PP
It is C11's and not v7's: a v7 program that wanted this wrote its own wrapper
round
.IR exit ,
and several in cmd/ still do.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
exit(2), fclose(3)
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/atexit.c
.br
lib/libc/gen/cuexit.c \- the pointer
.I exit
tests
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS Registering costs one word to everyone else
.I Exit
is tail-jumped to by
.I crt0
and is therefore in every program.  If it named the handler table outright, every
program would carry the 32 words of it, whether or not it had ever called
.IR atexit .
So
.I exit
tests a pointer that
.I atexit
arms \- the same bargain
.IR fclose (3)
makes for the stdio flush, and for the same reason.
.I Hello
pays one word of bss for this page and nothing else.
.SH BUGS
.B The table is 32 entries and the limit is not diagnosed at compile time.
A thirty-third registration simply returns \-1, and a caller that ignores the
result loses the handler silently.
